## Independent derivations

### 161

All solutions below are real.

a) Factor $x^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y)$.
For $x=2y$, the second equation becomes $5y^2=20$, so $(x,y)=(4,2),(-4,-2)$.
For $x=-2y$, it becomes $y^2=20$, giving
$(-4\sqrt5,2\sqrt5),(4\sqrt5,-2\sqrt5)$.
Both factors have been exhausted.

b) $x=\pm3y/2$. For $x=3y/2$, the second equation is $-y^2/4=-1$,
giving $(-3,-2),(3,2)$. The other branch is $11y^2/4=-1$, with no real roots.

c) Put $p=xy$. Then $p^2+p-72=(p-8)(p+9)=0$.
With $x+y=6$, $p=8$ yields $x,y=2,4$ in both orders;
$p=-9$ yields $x,y=3\pm3\sqrt2$ in both orders.

d) Put $s=x+y$, $p=xy$. Then $(s-5)(s+3)=0$ and $p=11-s$.
$s=5,p=6$ yields $(2,3),(3,2)$.
$s=-3,p=14$ has discriminant $9-56=-47$, so no real solution.

### 162

Use original numerator $p$ and denominator $q$; do not silently reduce them.

a) Require $q,q+3,q+6\ne0$.
$(p+2)/(q+3)=p/q$ gives $2q=3p$, hence original value $2/3$.
The second equation gives $(p+1)/(q+6)=1/2$, hence $2p-q=4$.
Together these yield $q=12,p=8$, with all denominators allowed.

b) Require $q,q+3,q+1\ne0$.
First equation gives $q=2p-1$.
The product condition gives $5p(p-1)=q(q+1)$, hence $p(p-3)=0$.
$p=0,q=-1$ is inadmissible because $q+1=0$.
The only valid original fraction is $p=3,q=5$.
This also exhausts nonpositive algebraic candidates, without assuming them away.

### 163

a) Positive sides $u,v$ satisfy $u^2+v^2=289,uv=120$.
Thus $u+v=23$ and $\{u,v\}=\{8,15\}$ dm.

b) Positive legs satisfy $u^2+v^2=676,uv=240$.
Thus $u+v=34$, giving legs 10 and 24 cm, perimeter $34+26=60$ cm.

### 164

a) Original legs $u,v>0$ satisfy $u^2+v^2=169$ and
$(u+3)^2+(v+3)^2=289$.
Subtracting gives $6(u+v)+18=120$, hence $u+v=17$, $uv=60$,
and legs 5,12 m (up to exchange).
Area increase is $[3(u+v)+9]/2=30$ square metres.

b) Smaller legs $u,v>0$ satisfy $u^2+v^2=25$ and
$(u+17)^2+(v+17)^2=841$.
Subtracting gives $34(u+v)+578=816$, so $u+v=7$, $uv=12$.
The legs are 3,4 dm and 20,21 dm.
Area difference is $[17(u+v)+289]/2=204$ square decimetres.

### 165

a) Full-pool times $X,Y>0$ obey $1/X+1/Y=1/40$ and $X+2Y=234$.
Elimination gives $Y^2-137Y+4680=(Y-65)(Y-72)=0$.
Both $(X,Y)=(104,65),(90,72)$ satisfy both original equations and positivity.

b) Full-field times obey $1/X+1/Y=1/20$ and $X/2-Y/3=3$.
Then $Y=(3X-18)/2$, and
$3X^2-118X+360=(X-36)(3X-10)=0$.
$X=36,Y=45$ is valid. $X=10/3,Y=-4$ is invalid.
The negative candidate is recorded rather than hidden.

### 166

a) Let speeds be $u,v>0$, and let the first person's pre-meeting time be $t$.
Pre-meeting distances are $ut$ and $v(t-1)$, differing by 2 km.
The first post-meeting time is $5/2$ h, so $v(t-1)=5u/2$.
The second arrives $1/10$ h earlier, so its post-meeting time is $12/5$ h,
and $ut=12v/5=5u/2+2$. Thus $t=5/2+2/u$.
Substitute into the distance relation to obtain
$9u^2-32u-16=(u-4)(9u+4)=0$.
Discard $u=-4/9$. The valid values are $u=4,v=5$ km/h and distance 22 km.
Pre-meeting distances 12 and 10 km; post-meeting times 2.5 and 2.4 h.
Absolute arrival times, from the first departure, are 5.5 and 5.4 h.

b) Bus speed $u$, motorcycle speed $v$, bus pre-meeting time $t$.
Motorcycle starts $1/2$ h later. Its post-meeting time is 1 h;
the bus's is $3/2$ h. Hence $ut=v$ and $v(t-1/2)=3u/2$.
Eliminate $t$: $(v-3u/2)(v+u)=0$.
Positive speeds force $v=3u/2$. Total distance $v+3u/2=90$ gives
$u=30,v=45$ km/h. The $v=-u$ branch is physically inadmissible.

### 167

a) $u+v=112/(14/15)=120$ km/h; A's train is faster.
$112/v-112/u=1/4$ h gives $448(u-v)=uv$.
With $v=120-u$ this factors as $(u-64)(u+840)=0$.
The only positive-speed pair is $u=64,v=56$ km/h.

b) Common pre-meeting time $t>0$ satisfies $vt/u=1/3$ and $ut/v=3/4$.
Multiplication gives $t^2=1/4$, hence $t=1/2$ h (not the negative root).
Thus $u/v=3/2$, and $u+v=80/t=160$, giving $u=96,v=64$ km/h.

### 168

This is a polynomial coefficient task, not a physical word problem; no
positivity restriction on coefficients is present in the literal text.

a) Coefficients of the product are
$(a,1+ab,-3+b+4a,4-3b,-12)$.
Therefore $ab=-10,b+4a=3,c=4-3b$.
$4a^2-3a-10=(a-2)(4a+5)=0$.
Both triples $(-5/4,8,-20)$ and $(2,-5,19)$ are valid.

b) Product coefficients are $(3,-11,c+6+3q,-3c-2q,cq)$.
Thus $3c+2q=0,cq=-24,n=c+6+3q$.
This gives $c^2=16$, hence $(n,c,q)=(-8,4,-6)$ or $(20,-4,6)$.
Neither branch may be removed.

### 169

a) Let equal leg length be $L$ and base be $B$, in dm; $2L+B=140$.
The rhombus shares adjacent sides with a base edge and a leg, so those
sides meet at a base vertex. At height fraction $24/L$ of the triangle,
the line parallel to the base has length $B(1-24/L)=24$.
Equivalently $24/B+24/L=1$.
Eliminate B to get $L^2-82L+1680=(L-40)(L-42)=0$.
Both $(L,B)=(40,60),(42,56)$ obey $B>0,L>24,B>24,B<2L$;
both have valid nondegenerate triangles and rhombi. No figure was imagined.

b) At height $h$ cm, similarity gives width $w=120(1-h/60)=120-2h$.
$h(120-2h)=1750$, hence
$h^2-60h+875=(h-25)(h-35)=0$.
Both heights are between 0 and 60.
$(h,w)=(25,70)$ gives perimeter 190 cm; $(35,50)$ gives 170 cm.

### 170-174

170: rearrange the linear inequalities; non-strict signs include all
boundaries. Absolute-value vertices are (0,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,0).
Candidate records rays, shading directions and intercepts per subpart.

171: slope through (0,0),(5,1) is 1/5.
Open upper half-plane: $y>x/5$. Closed upper half-plane: $y\geq x/5$.
Literal text omits an explicit boundary convention; candidate separates
these, and independent text review must settle the intended convention.

172: complete square in c to $(x+2)^2$. a/c include their parabolas;
b has a strict sign and excludes the entire downward parabola.

173: division by x reverses inequality only when deriving from xy.
Consequently a/b and c/d are not interchangeable. a/c exclude x=0 by
original denominator domain; d includes every point on x=0 and both axes.
Candidate records each sign branch and which hyperbola side is shaded.

174: y=4 and y=x^2+6 are concrete witnesses.
All nonhorizontal lines fail full containment. For a vertical-axis parabola
$y=ax^2+bx+c$, full containment above y=5 requires $a>0$ and its minimum
$c-b^2/(4a)>5$. Other correct witnesses are allowed; fixed-expression
grading would misjudge them, so this task is ungraded.

### 175-180

175: distance $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\leq7$ is equivalent to
$x^2+y^2\leq49$ since distance and 7 are nonnegative. The circle is included.

176: nonnegative square sum gives respectively the whole plane, the punctured
plane, and the singleton origin.

178: $|x|<3.7$ is an open vertical strip.
$|y|\geq5.5$ gives two closed horizontal half-planes, not the middle strip.

179: a is the closed unit diamond; b is the exterior plus boundary of
the radius-4 diamond. For c all x<=0 are allowed; for x>0 the right open
wedge $-x<y<x$ is removed. d is below the slope-2 upward V, including it.

180: diamond diagonal lengths 4 and 4 give area 8 square units.

### 181-184

181a: $0<y<4$ and $y/2<x<y$; the three boundary intersections are
(0,0),(2,4),(4,4), all excluded.
181b: $4-x<y<x+2$ demands x>1; combine with x<4 to get an open triangle
with vertices (1,3),(4,0),(4,6), all excluded.
181c: $x\geq0$, $7/2\leq x+y\leq6$, an unbounded closed half-strip.
181d: $y+3\leq x\leq5-y$ implies y<=1; with y>=1 only (4,1) survives.
181e: closed rectangle of width 4, height 6.
181f: closed vertical strip intersected with two outside horizontal regions.

182: substitution into the equality gives closed rays
$(t,t),t\geq0$; $(-t,t),t\geq0$; $(t,t),t\geq-1$.

183a/b: same-sign/opposite-sign closed quadrants, axes included.
183c: translate the sign chart to (2,3), with both complete zero-factor lines.
183d: $x^2-y^2\leq0$ is $|y|\geq|x|$.
183e: domain xy!=0; expression is $(x+y)^2/(xy)>0$.
It holds exactly for xy>0; same-sign nonzero x,y imply x+y!=0.
183f: domain xy!=0; expression is $-(x-y)^2/(xy)>0$.
It holds exactly for xy<0; opposite-sign nonzero x,y imply x-y!=0.
Zeros on axes are never restored after algebraic cancellation.

184: interval for y is $[3x+b,3x+1]$.
b<1 gives a positive-width closed band; b=1 gives one line;
b>1 gives the empty set. Normal distance is $(1-b)/sqrt(1+3^2)$.

### 185

a: squared-radius bounds 1.69 and 9 give radii 1.3 and 3; both circles included.
b: radius 6 disk restricted to |x|>1; exclude both vertical chords and
their four endpoints, include allowed arcs and the extreme points (±6,0).
c: radius 10 disk restricted to |x|<8; exclude x=±8 and the four endpoints
(±8,±6), include allowed arcs.
d: radius 1.2 disk restricted to xy>=0; first/third closed quarter-disks.
e: radii 8 through 10, xy<=0; second/fourth closed quarter-annuli;
include their axis segments, not the origin.
f: compatibility $2x^2\leq9$ gives $|x|\leq3/\sqrt2$,
and crossing y=9/2; both parabolic boundaries included.
g: $(x+2)^2\leq4$ gives -4<=x<=0, crossings (-4,4),(0,4).
h: $(x-1)^2\leq7/2$ gives $1-\sqrt{14}/2\leq x\leq1+\sqrt{14}/2$.
i: $(x-2)(x-3)\leq0$ gives 2<=x<=3 and vertex (5/2,-1/4).
All f-i endpoints and boundary segments are included.
All original expressions in 185 are polynomial/absolute-value expressions;
radical boundary descriptions do not change their original domains.

### 187

a: triangle (0,0),(-4,4),(4,4), area $8\cdot4/2=16$ square units.
b: solve the line against each V ray: intersections (-1,1) and (3,3).
With origin the determinant is -6, hence area 3 square units.
Alternatively split at x=0: two triangles have areas 3/4 and 9/4.
These are computed values, not unevaluated area formulas.

## Verification and limitations

`verify.py` uses only Python standard-library exact fractions and a small
quadratic-number ring. It checks the equations, domain rejection,
positive-time roots, all retained branches, coefficient convolution,
geometric relations, rational-grid region equivalences and exact areas.
Grid tests support sign/boundary checks; they are not a proof of completeness.
The algebraic factorizations above supply completeness arguments.

`verify-math.mjs` reuses the existing local ComputeEngine and the repository's
numeric/expression validator to check that every numerical key is parseable
and finite. It does not import the server function or connect to a database.
It is not a browser or MathLive edit check.

Read-only discovery failures are retained verbatim in `tool-receipts.json`.
No missing virtualenv or missing guessed checker path is a content failure;
neither caused a dependency installation.

Executed receipts:

- `verification-algebra-001.json`: exit 0, 113092 exact checks, no failures;
  25 exercises, 75 primary source subparts, 13 auto/12 ungraded exercises,
  72 auto fields, 50 ungraded display slots. The extra slot in 171 separates
  the requested line from its upper-half-plane description.
- `verification-latex-001.json`: exit 0; all 69 numeric keys parse and
  evaluate to finite real values; all 12 exact alternatives parse.
  Parsing the alternatives is not a MathLive edit or exact judging test.
- Both verification receipts bind candidate SHA-256
  `4f2d71f212be69335efcc83a174915f2cb7235f7328719fd3883e03bf87fe3bb`.
- Graph checks concern the authored analytic regions, not an image render
  or a product browser submission. No application acceptance was claimed.

